  "body": {
    "id": "prop:additive-reduction",
    "kind": "proposition",
    "statement": "When \\(k=1\\), the criterion reduces to \\(\\Phi_1(d)=1+\\sum_{i=1}^p r(d_i)\\), and the optimizer reduces to the uniform limited-supply dosage in the additive PFED setting. When \\(L\\ge p/2\\), it further reduces to half dosage with \\(\\Phi^\\star=1+p\\).",
    "free_symbols": [
      "k",
      "Phi",
      "d",
      "p",
      "r",
      "L",
      "PhiStar"
    ],
    "depends_on": [
      "lem:trace-identity",
      "thm:uniform-optimum"
    ],
    "justification": "This is the required sanity reduction to the known additive and unconstrained designs.",
    "gap": "The reduction is known from ShyamalZhangUhler2025; it is included as a fidelity check rather than a novelty claim.",
    "consumer": "Readers can verify that the all-order theorem preserves the established additive benchmark.",
    "proof_tex": "When \\(k=1\\), the retained family consists only of the empty set and the \\(p\\) singletons. The trace identity immediately becomes\n\\[\n \\Phi_1(d)=1+\\sum_{i=1}^p r(d_i).\n\\]\nThe uniform-optimum theorem gives the unique optimizer \\(d_i=u=\\min\\{L/p,1/2\\}\\), which is the uniform limited-supply dosage in the additive product-Bernoulli design. If \\(L\\ge p/2\\), then \\(u=1/2\\) and \\(r(1/2)=1\\), so\n\\[\n \\Phi^\\star=1+p.\n\\]",
